---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Bayesian Fine-Mapping, SuSiE & Statistical Colocalization";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical derivation of the LD confounding equation, Wakefield's Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF) with null normalization, the Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE) variational framework, 95% Credible Sets, and statistical colocalization.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 820 prose words, 8 display and 55 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "9 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 6</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Mathematical</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      When GWAS identifies a significant genomic locus, it typically contains hundreds of highly correlated SNPs spanning tens of kilobases. Which specific single nucleotide change is the true causal driver, and which are merely non-causal passenger variants hitchhiking on shared haplotypes? This deep dive explores the mathematical machinery of <strong>statistical fine-mapping</strong>: the <strong>LD confounding matrix equation</strong>, <strong>Wakefield's Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF)</strong> with null-model normalization, the <strong>Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE)</strong> variational framework, <strong>95% Credible Sets</strong>, functional priors via <strong>PolyFun</strong>, and <strong>colocalization (coloc)</strong> between GWAS and molecular QTLs.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#confounding-eq">1. The Fundamental LD Confounding Matrix Equation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#wakefield">2. Wakefield's Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF) & Null Model Normalization</a></li>
        <li><a href="#susie">3. Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE, Wang et al. 2020)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ibss">4. The IBSS Coordinate Ascent Variational Algorithm</a></li>
        <li><a href="#credible-sets">5. Calibrated 95% Credible Sets & Purity Diagnostics</a></li>
        <li><a href="#functional-priors">6. Informed Functional Priors: PolyFun</a></li>
        <li><a href="#coloc">7. Statistical Colocalization (coloc H0–H4) & TWAS Caveats</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">8. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="confounding-eq">
      <h2>1. The Fundamental LD Confounding Matrix Equation</h2>
      <p>
        In a fine-mapping locus containing <Katex tex="M" /> variants, let <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{true}} \in \mathbb{R}^M" /> be the true causal effect vector (which is sparse, having non-zero entries only at causal variants).
      </p>
      <p>
        Because of Linkage Disequilibrium, the marginal Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) GWAS effect estimates <Katex tex="\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}} \in \mathbb{R}^M" /> are corrupted by the <Katex tex="M \times M" /> LD correlation matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{R}" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">LD Confounding Matrix Equation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}] = \mathbf{R} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{true}}, \qquad \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{R} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{true}}, \frac{\sigma^2}{N} \mathbf{R}\right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{R}_{jk} = r_{jk}" /> is the pairwise LD correlation between SNP <Katex tex="j" /> and SNP <Katex tex="k" />. Even if only one variant is causal (<Katex tex="\beta_1 \ne 0" />), all neighboring variants in LD (<Katex tex="r_{1k} \ne 0" />) will show statistically significant marginal associations!
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="wakefield">
      <h2>2. Wakefield's Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF) & Null Model Normalization</h2>
      <p>
        Jon Wakefield (2009) derived an asymptotic approximation for the Bayes Factor of a single variant without requiring raw individual genotypes:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Wakefield Approximate Bayes Factor (ABF)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{ABF}_j = \text{BF}_{01, j} = \sqrt{\frac{V_j + W}{V_j}} \exp\left(-\frac{z_j^2}{2} \frac{W}{V_j + W}\right), \qquad \text{BF}_{10, j} = \frac{1}{\text{ABF}_j}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="z_j = \hat{\beta}_j / \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)" /> = Marginal GWAS Z-score &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_j = \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)^2" /> = Measurement error variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="W = \sigma_0^2" /> = Prior variance on the true effect size (typically <Katex tex="W = 0.04" /> for quantitative traits or <Katex tex="0.2^2" /> for log-odds).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Null-Inclusive Posterior Inclusion Probability (PIP)</h3>
      <p>
        Under the assumption of <strong>at most one causal variant</strong> in the locus, the Posterior Inclusion Probability (<Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j" />) that variant <Katex tex="j" /> is causal must normalize over the null model (<Katex tex="\mathcal{M}_0" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Null-Normalized Posterior Inclusion Probability (P0 Formulation)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j = P(\gamma_j = 1 \mid \mathcal{D}) = \frac{\pi_j \text{BF}_{10, j}}{\pi_0 + \sum_{k=1}^{M} \pi_k \text{BF}_{10, k}} = \frac{\pi_j / \text{ABF}_j}{\pi_0 + \sum_{k=1}^{M} \pi_k / \text{ABF}_k}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\pi_j" /> = Prior probability that SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is causal &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\pi_0 = 1 - \sum \pi_k" /> = Prior probability that no variant in the locus is causal.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="susie">
      <h2>3. Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE, Wang et al. 2020)</h2>
      <p>
        In complex loci harboring <strong>multiple distinct causal variants</strong>, single-variant ABF fails. <strong>SuSiE (Sum of Single Effects, Wang, Sarkar, Carbonetto & Stephens 2020)</strong> decomposes the multivariate regression model into a sum of <Katex tex="L" /> single-effect components:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">SuSiE Generative Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \mathbf{X} \boldsymbol{\gamma}_l b_l + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \boldsymbol{\gamma}_l \sim \operatorname{Multinomial}(1, \boldsymbol{\pi}), \quad b_l \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_l^2), \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 \mathbf{I})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\gamma}_l \in \{0, 1\}^M" /> is a selection vector with exactly one non-zero entry designating the causal variant for effect <Katex tex="l" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="b_l \in \mathbb{R}" /> is its effect magnitude.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="ibss">
      <h2>4. The IBSS Coordinate Ascent Variational Algorithm</h2>
      <p>
        SuSiE fits the posterior distribution via <strong>Iterative Bayesian Stepwise Selection (IBSS)</strong>, an exact Coordinate Ascent Variational Inference (CAVI) algorithm that maximizes the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">IBSS Residual Update & Single-Effect Posterior</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{r}_{-l} = \mathbf{y} - \sum_{l' \ne l} \mathbf{X} \hat{\mathbf{b}}_{l'}, \qquad \alpha_{lj} = P(\gamma_{lj} = 1 \mid \mathbf{r}_{-l}) = \frac{\pi_j \text{BF}(\mathbf{r}_{-l}, \mathbf{x}_j)}{\sum_{k=1}^M \pi_k \text{BF}(\mathbf{r}_{-l}, \mathbf{x}_k)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          IBSS iteratively computes the partial residual <Katex tex="\mathbf{r}_{-l}" /> subtracting all other <Katex tex="L - 1" /> effects, fits a single-effect regression, and updates posterior weights <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_l" /> until convergence.
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The overall marginal Posterior Inclusion Probability for SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j = 1 - \prod_{l=1}^{L} (1 - \alpha_{lj})" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="credible-sets">
      <h2>5. Calibrated 95% Credible Sets & Purity Diagnostics</h2>
      <p>
        For each single-effect component <Katex tex="l \in \{1, \dots, L\}" />, SuSiE constructs a <strong><Katex tex="\rho" />-Credible Set (<Katex tex="\text{CS}_l" />)</strong> by ranking variants in descending order of posterior probability <Katex tex="\alpha_{lj}" /> and including the minimum number of SNPs necessary to satisfy:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Credible Set Condition & Purity Threshold</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\sum_{j \in \text{CS}_l} \alpha_{lj} \ge 0.95, \qquad \operatorname{Purity}(\text{CS}_l) = \min_{j, k \in \text{CS}_l} |r_{jk}| \ge 0.50" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>Purity Diagnostic:</strong> Ensures that all variants within the credible set are in strong mutual Linkage Disequilibrium (<Katex tex="|r| \ge 0.5" />). If purity is low, the set may represent an unresolvable mixture of discordant signals.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="functional-priors">
      <h2>6. Informed Functional Priors: PolyFun</h2>
      <p>
        Standard fine-mapping assumes a uniform prior (<Katex tex="\pi_j = 1 / M" />). However, non-coding causal variants are overwhelmingly concentrated in functional elements (e.g. active enhancers, promoters, conserved sequences).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>PolyFun (Weissbrod et al. 2020 Nature Genetics)</strong> uses Stratified LD Score Regression (S-LDSC) coefficients (<Katex tex="\tau_c" />) across functional annotations to compute variant-specific prior causal probabilities:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">PolyFun Empirical Prior Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\pi_j \propto \exp\left(\sum_{c=1}^{C} \tau_c a_{jc}\right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="a_{jc} \in \{0, 1\}" /> indicates whether SNP <Katex tex="j" /> carries functional annotation <Katex tex="c" />. PolyFun increases fine-mapping resolution by over 30% while maintaining well-calibrated false discovery rates.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="coloc">
      <h2>7. Statistical Colocalization (coloc H0–H4) & TWAS Caveats</h2>
      <p>
        When a GWAS association locus overlaps an expression Quantitative Trait Locus (eQTL), <strong>colocalization (coloc, Giambartolomei et al. 2014)</strong> evaluates five mutually exclusive posterior hypotheses:
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Hypothesis</th>
              <th>Biological Configuration</th>
              <th>Posterior Threshold</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_0" /></strong></td>
              <td>No association with either GWAS trait 1 or eQTL trait 2.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="P(H_0 \mid \mathcal{D})" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_1" /></strong></td>
              <td>Association with GWAS trait 1 only; no eQTL effect.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="P(H_1 \mid \mathcal{D})" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_2" /></strong></td>
              <td>Association with eQTL trait 2 only; no GWAS disease effect.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="P(H_2 \mid \mathcal{D})" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_3" /></strong></td>
              <td>Both traits associated, but driven by <strong>two distinct causal variants</strong> in LD (<Katex tex="r^2 \ne 1" />).</td>
              <td><Katex tex="P(H_3 \mid \mathcal{D})" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="H_4" /></strong></td>
              <td>Both traits share a <strong>single common causal variant</strong>.</td>
              <td><strong><Katex tex="P(H_4 \mid \mathcal{D}) > 0.80" /></strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> TWAS & Colocalization: Prioritization vs. Causality
        </div>
        <p>
          Transcriptome-Wide Association Studies (TWAS, e.g. PrediXcan, FUSION) impute gene expression from genotypes into GWAS cohorts.
          <br/>
          <strong>Critical Methodological Caveat:</strong> A significant TWAS hit or <Katex tex="P(H_4) > 0.8" /> colocalization prioritizes a candidate gene, but <strong>does not prove that the gene causally mediates the disease</strong>!
          Co-regulation of adjacent genes in high LD and pleiotropic transcription factor binding can generate false-positive TWAS associations (Wainberg et al. 2019). Definitive proof requires functional perturbation (e.g. CRISPRi/CRISPRa assays).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>8. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>LD Confounding:</strong> <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}] = \mathbf{R}\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{true}}" /> distorts marginal effect sizes across correlated variants.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Wakefield ABF:</strong> Computes single-variant Bayes factors; requires null normalization <Katex tex="\pi_0" /> for valid PIPs.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>SuSiE & IBSS:</strong> Decomposes complex multi-causal loci via variational optimization, outputting pure 95% Credible Sets.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Colocalization:</strong> Tests shared causality (<Katex tex="H_4" />) vs LD hitchhiking (<Katex tex="H_3" />) between GWAS and eQTLs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: Rare Variant Association Testing (SKAT & DeepRVAT)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 7 to discover how to overcome the single-variant power cliff using collapsing burden tests, Sequence Kernel Association Tests (SKAT/SKAT-O), and deep neural aggregation with DeepRVAT.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-rare-variant-association/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 7: Rare Variant Association</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
